      "rationale": "The report identifies the regressed behaviour as \"format reindent: stmts\": formatting multiple semicolon-separated statements with reindent=True. The library's own split() (documented in docs/source/intro.rst) treats \"select foo; select bar\" as two independent statements with no blank line implied between them, and sqlparse's well-established reindent behaviour simply places each re-indented statement on its own line separated by a single newline (e.g. 'select foo;\\nselect bar'), never inserting extra blank lines for plain, single-line statements. Tracing sqlparse/engine/statement_splitter.py and sqlparse/filters/reindent.py shows that the trailing newline consumed after the terminating ';' is attached to the *next* statement and then stripped to an empty token by StripWhitespaceFilter; ReindentFilter._split_statements then mistakes that now-empty leading whitespace token for \"not the first token\" and inserts a spurious extra newline, on top of the '\\n\\n' that ReindentFilter.process() already inserts because the previous statement never ends with '\\n' \u2014 together producing multiple blank lines instead of the single expected newline, which is what the two tests assert against.",
